THE TYPESETTER C TOOLCHAIN ON STOCK V6
=====================================

The directories here turn a stock Sixth Edition system into one
with the "typesetter" C compiler and the tools that go with it,
built from source with nothing but the V6 system itself.  At the
end the system has:

	/lib/c0 c1 c2	the typesetter C compiler (long, unsigned,
			typedef, initializers with =)
	/bin/cc		the UNSW driver (#if expressions, #else,
			-D, -I, -E, nested includes)
	/bin/as		the UNSW assembler (-o, -u)
	/lib/libc.a	the V6 C library, plus what the new
			compiler needs (long * / %, new cret, ...)
	/bin/ar /bin/ld	the new archive format (magic 0177545,
			14 character names), all libraries converted
	/lib/libS.a	the UNSW standard I/O library (stdio.h)

This is the base that the 1BSD software expects.

All sources come unchanged from the UNSW 3 tape.  Where a change
is needed, run makes it with ed on a copy, as tc does.


DIRECTORIES, IN THE ORDER THEY MUST BE INSTALLED

	tc	the compiler passes c0 c1 c2 (see tc/READ_ME)
	libc	new members for /lib/libc.a
	as	the assembler
	cc	the driver
	ar	ar, ld, file and arcv; converts every library
	stdio	libS.a and /usr/include/stdio.h

Each directory has two scripts:

	run	builds and tests, changes nothing outside
		this directory (except /usr/tcboot)
	install	installs the result, after saving what it
		replaces

Why this order:

- libc comes first after tc because the new compiler generates
  calls to lmul, ldiv, lrem, almul, aldiv and alrem, and its
  functions that return a long need the new cret (see NOTES).
  cc and arcv also need mktemp, and ar needs itol.
- the UNSW cc calls "as -u -o file.o", which the V6 as does not
  accept, so as must be installed before cc.  The UNSW as still
  accepts the V6 "as -", so the tc driver keeps working.
- ar.c, arcv.c and ld.c use long, so they need the new compiler
  and the new libc.  ar must switch everything at once, see ar
  below.
- stdio needs -I from the UNSW cc, lseek and _exit from the new
  libc, and is archived with the new ar.


BEFORE YOU START

1. Back up the disk images.  Each install changes /bin and /lib.

2. Log in as root.  Run every script from its own directory.

3. Free space, as measured on a stock system with type_C in
   /usr/work (a separate RP02):
	/ and /usr (rk0)	about 650 blocks for tc, then about
				130 more for the rest
	/usr/work		about 750 blocks for the sources,
				about 1050 more for the builds; ar
				keeps a copy of every library it
				converts in ar/save

4. Getting the files in.  On the host, from the top of the
   repository, write the tape with:

	toolbox/mkv6tap.py type_C work.tap

   The first file on the tape is a tar that runs on V6, so pull
   it out with dd first, then let it extract everything (it makes
   the directories itself).  For example, with the tape on tm0
   and type_C going into /usr/work:

	chdir /usr/work
	dd if=/dev/mt0 of=tar bs=512 skip=1 count=49
	chmod 755 tar
	./tar x

   mkv6tap.py prints the dd command with the right count.  Unlike
   tp, the tape has no limit on the number of files.


INSTALLING

For each directory in the order above:

	chdir /usr/work/type_C/<directory>
	sh run

Check the output as described below, and only then:

	sh install

Lines like "c00.c:" and the numbers printed by ed are normal.


tc

See tc/READ_ME.  run must print nothing between "any output
between here and done means failure" and "done".


libc

run copies /lib/libc.a to save/libc.a (only the first time), and
builds new/libc.a from it, adding or replacing:

	lseek.o		32 bit seek, put before seek.o
	mktemp.o	put before stat.o
	lops.o		lmul ldiv lrem almul aldiv alrem
			(longops.s), put before csv.o
	itol.o		put before csv.o
	csv.o		new cret, which leaves r0 and r1 alone
	exit.o		calls __cleanup before exiting
	clean.o		a dummy __cleanup, at the end
	_exit.o		exit without __cleanup, at the end

The order is worked out by ed from "ar t" of the saved library,
so a libc.a that already differs from the stock one is fine.
Names are at most 8 characters, the limit of the V6 archive.

run ends by compiling ltest.c with the new library and running
it.  It must print

	long ops ok

install copies new/libc.a to /lib/libc.a.
To undo: cp save/libc.a /lib/libc.a


as

run assembles the UNSW assembler with the V6 one (stage1, in
/usr/tcboot/as), then again with stage1 (stage2), using -u and
-o.  The two must be identical: nothing may be printed between
"any output between here and done means failure" and "done".
stage1 uses a copy of as11.s that runs /usr/tcboot/as/as2; the
final as1 runs /lib/as2.

install saves /bin/as and /lib/as2 in /usr/tcboot/orig and
installs as1 and as2 there.
To undo: copy /usr/tcboot/orig/as and as2 back.


cc

run compiles cc.c with the tc driver, then compiles cc.c again
with the result.  The two must be identical.  Then it compiles
and runs ../libc/ltest.c with the new driver: long ops ok.

install replaces /bin/cc.
To undo: cp /usr/tcboot/cc /bin/cc (the tc driver), or
/usr/tcboot/orig/cc (the V6 driver).


ar

The new ar and ld read only the new archive format, and the old
ones read only the old format, so the programs and all the
libraries must change together.

run compiles ar (with the changes in NOTES), arcv, ld and file,
then checks them without touching the system:

- converts copies of libc.a and liba.a into new, and compares
  "ar t" of the old and the converted copies
- links ld with the new ld and the converted copies; the result
  must be identical to ld
- links ltest with them: long ops ok
- if /usr/sys/conf/c.o exists, links a kernel twice, with the
  old ld and lib1, lib2 and with the new ld and converted
  copies; the two must be identical

install saves in save the old ar, ld, file and every library,
converts in place

	/lib/libc.a liba.a libf.a filib.a libp.a libs.a liby.a
	/lib/tmgb /usr/lib/tmgb
	/usr/sys/lib1 /usr/sys/lib2

installs ar, ld and file in /bin and arcv in /usr/bin, and runs
ltest again.  arcv says "not archive format" for a file that is
already converted, so running install twice is harmless.

Convert any other archive of your own with arcv.  aredit (../vanilla/aredit)
only understands the old format.

To undo: copy everything in save back where it came from (tmgb
to /lib/tmgb, utmgb to /usr/lib/tmgb, lib1 and lib2 to /usr/sys).


stdio

run compiles everything with -I. (so it does not need
/usr/include yet), and builds libS.a with each member before
the members it uses.  ftell.o and strlen.o are left out, see
NOTES.  It then runs stest.c, with its output sent to a file, so
that the test also shows that exit flushes the buffers.  It must
print

	stdio ok

install copies libS.a to /lib/libS.a and stdio.h to
/usr/include/stdio.h (making /usr/include), and runs stest again,
now with cc -lS.


NOTES

- The new compiler expects a cret that keeps r0 and r1, as the
  long result of a function is returned in r0 and r1.  The V6
  cret uses r1 as a scratch register, so with the V6 libc a
  function returning a long loses the low word.  csv.s comes
  from UNSW s4; the old compiler does not care.

- The V6 ld reads a library once, from start to end.  A member
  is loaded only if it defines a symbol that is undefined at that
  point, so every member must come before the members it uses.

- The V6 sh looks for a command in the current directory first.
  In ar/run the old ar must be called as /bin/ar, or the new ar
  just built there is used instead.

- The V6 rm -r passes all the names of a directory as arguments
  and fails on large directories with "Arg list too long";
  libc/run removes each member as soon as it is archived.

- ar.c is compiled without AUSAM, which uses the UNSW newfstat
  system call and 16 bit user ids.  It also calls
  ctime(arbuf.date), passing the long itself; the V6 ctime takes
  a pointer, so run changes it to ctime(&arbuf.date).

- ftell needs the UNSW tell system call, which V6 does not have.

- strlen is already defined in perror.o of the V6 libc; with a
  second one in libS.a, ld reports _strlen multiply defined.  The
  _strlen used by libS comes from libc instead.

- __cleanup (stdio's flush on exit) is 9 characters; the
  assembler and the compiler both cut it to __cleanu.

